Browse is a screen-oriented directory browser, based on the metaphor of using'VI' to edit an 'ls -l' listing.Commands:	SPACE	Change directory (if cursor points to a directory).	SPACE	Page file through 'more' (if cursor points to a file).	x		Hex dump of current file or directory.	&		Run a program that won't clobber the screen.	$		Run a program.	=		Enter a directory name.	?		Page this help file through 'more'.	[		Define a macro.	H		Go to the top of the page.	J		Go to the bottom of the directory.	K		Go to the top of the directory.	L		Go to the bottom of the page.	^L		Repaint screen.	M		Display macros.	S		Save macros.	I		Install macros from current file.	.		Go down 20 lines.	o		Go up 20 lines.	R		Rename a file.	dd		Delete file(s). (ask for verification)	DD		Delete file(s).	h		Place cursor at beginning of line.	l		Move cursor to end of line.	<		Display file names only.	>		Display long directory listing.	,		Go down a line.	k,^K	Go up a line.	n,^D	Go down 11 lines.	p,^U	Go up 11 lines.	qq,QQ,ZZ	Exit.	r		Re-read directory.	t		Toggle tag on current file or directory.	T		Tag all files.	U		Untag all files or directories.Macros:	[xtext]	Define x as text.Within text:	%		Current filename(s).	#		Current directory.	!		Previous command.	$x		Macro for key x	$$		Current process ID	~		Home directory.	^K		One character from previous command.	@x		Execute macro for key x	\x		Enter special character x	\nnn	nnn, octal	\^x		Control-xDefault macros:	b		=..<CR>	~		=~<CR>	%		$%<CR>	SPACE	$more %<CR>	c		$copy % 	s		$scred %<CR>	When a command is executed (using '$<prog>'), the environment in the formpresent at start of 'browse' will automatically be passed to <prog>. Inaddition, the environment variable FILE will be set to the current file name.